  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 407 PM EDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis: \n\nA large-scale 500mb low pressure system over central Hudson Bay will \ncontinue to pivot embedded waves of energy across the Great Lakes \nRegion for the next few days. Current shortwave troughing pivoting \nacross mid/southern Michigan will deliver showers and thunderstorms \nacross the lower peninsula. Stronger forcing and instability remain \nto our south, embedded convection combined with favorable deep \nmoisture profiles could produce some embedded heavy rainfall through \nthis evening. \n\nThe surface-level boundary will continue to progress toward the east \ncoast, but lingering low-level moisture will keep chances of light \nprecipitation this Sunday. Midlevel flow begins to turn somewhat \nmore zonal for the Great Lakes Region beyond the midweek as \nprecipitation chances remain rather limited. Aforementioned large-\nscale closed low to the north continues to influence the \nMidwest as H8 temperatures drop around 10 degrees Celsius. \nSurface temperatures slightly below normal Thursday through next\nweekend are expected, with highs remaining in the low 70s for \nmost locations. \n\nForecast Details:\n\nTonight: Well-advertised showers and storms currently over Lake \nMichigan continue to make their way toward central and southern \nMichigan. The more ideal synoptic forcing remains to our south as \nstrong low-level jet dynamics iterate with ideal instability while \nsteady stratiform rainfall occupies most of northern lower this \nevening. While the majority of areas along and south of the M-32 \ncorridor can expect only a few hundredths to a quarter inch, model \nsoundings with tall skinny CAPE, PWATs approaching 2 inches and deep \nmoist profiles could produce heavy convection that could result in \nlocalized areas with much higher amounts through this evening.\n\nMonday and Tuesday: Lingering low-level moisture combined with weak \ncyclonic flow to the north will keep low chances of precipitation \nacross the Great Lakes Region this Monday and Tuesday. Monday will \nbe mostly dry as most convection remains to our south, but expect \none or two showers near the Saginaw Bay Region. Better chances of \nscattered showers remain on Tuesday as additional low-level \nshortwaves support afternoon convection. Conditions will be similar \nto Monday where the majority of the region will remain mostly sunny \nwith highs in the low 80s, but a few localized areas will likely \nobserve short lived convective driven showers. \n\nWednesday through Sunday Outlook: Midlevel heights lowering behind \nthese shortwaves will usher in a somewhat cooler air mass with dry \nCanadian air occupying the Great Lakes Region. H8 temperatures near \n10 degrees will support highs in the low to mid 70s. Expect mostly \ndry and pleasant weather supported by surface high pressure into \nnext weekend. Nighttime lows will drop into the upper 40s to low 50s \nacross interior northern lower and eastern upper.",
